fsck - check the filesystem

SYNOPSIS
    fsck [-y]

DESCRIPTION
    Two things on the disk describe which blocks are spoken for: the files
    themselves, by way of the extents that say where their contents live,
    and the block bitmaps, which the allocator reads when it needs
    somewhere to put something new. They should agree. Every place they do
    not is either a block that will be handed out to a second file while
    the first is still using it, or a block that is lost until the disk is
    emptied.

    fsck walks the tree, works out which blocks and inodes are genuinely
    in use, and compares that against the bitmaps and against the free
    counts the superblock carries.

        fsck        # look, and say
        fsck -y     # look, and correct what can be corrected

    Without -y nothing is written. With it, blocks that belong to a file
    but are marked free are marked used, and the superblock's free count
    is recomputed. Repairing needs root; looking does not.

    A block marked used that nothing refers to is reported but not freed.
    It wastes space and cannot cause loss, and reclaiming it on the
    strength of a walk this simple is a worse risk than leaving it.

    The exit status is 0 when clean, 1 when problems were found and
    repaired, and 4 when problems were found and left alone - the
    convention a boot script expects.

BUGS
    The check runs inside the filesystem server, because that is the only
    thing that knows the layout, and a second implementation of the ext4
    reader that disagreed with the first would be a worse problem than the
    one this is for. The cost is real: this cannot examine a filesystem
    that failed to mount, which is the case a checker is most wanted for,
    and it sees the disk as the server currently believes it to be rather
    than as it is.

    Blocks are found by walking the extent mapping, not the structure
    that holds it. Two things follow. A file large enough to need a
    second level of extent tree has that index block reported as lost -
    no file on an image this size is. And the resize inode, which keeps
    its reserved blocks in the older indirect format rather than in
    extents, is not walked at all; the one block this reports as
    reachable from nothing on a freshly made image is its.

    Link counts, duplicate entries and orphaned inodes are not checked.

SEE ALSO
    df, mount, stat
